.Dd August 13, 2026
.Dt YPSTNS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ypstns
.Nd serve an STNS directory as YP
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dnv
.Op Fl D Ar macro Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a YP server that answers out of an
.Lk https://stns.jp/ STNS
API server, so that
.Xr getpwnam 3 ,
.Xr getgrent 3
and everything built on them resolve users and groups from a directory rather
than from
.Xr master.passwd 5 .
.Pp
.Ox
has no
.Xr nsswitch.conf 5
and no pluggable directory backend of any other kind.
What it has is YP, which libc has spoken since forever and which
.Xr getpwnam 3
consults whenever
.Pa /etc/master.passwd
holds a
.Sq +
line.
That is the same reason
.Xr ypldap 8
is in the base system, and
.Nm
is built the same way for the same reasons.
.Pp
It runs as two processes.
The privileged one holds the maps and answers the RPC; it stays root because
.Sy master.passwd.byname
exists and serving it correctly means knowing whether the request came from a
reserved port.
The other runs as
.Sy _ypstns
behind
.Xr unveil 2
and
.Xr pledge 2 ,
does the HTTP, and sends finished map entries back over a pipe.
Neither can do the other's job, and the privileged one parses nothing: the RPC
decoding is libc's and the text of the maps is assembled by the fetcher.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl D Ar macro Ns = Ns Ar value
Set
.Ar macro
to
.Ar value
before reading the configuration file.
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize, and write every log line to standard error as well.
.It Fl f Ar file
Read
.Ar file
instead of
.Pa /etc/ypstns.conf .
.It Fl n
Read the configuration, print what was made of it, and exit.
Needs no privileges, which is the point: it is meant to be run on a file before
it is installed.
.It Fl v
Log more.
Give it twice to hear about macros that were set and never used.
.El
.Ss Setting it up
.Nm
provides the server half only.
The client half is
.Xr ypbind 8 ,
in the base system, and the machine has to be told to consult it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# echo stns > /etc/defaultdomain
# domainname stns
# echo '+:*::::::::' >> /etc/master.passwd
# echo '+:*::' >> /etc/group
# pwd_mkdb /etc/master.passwd
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Sq +
lines are what make libc ask YP at all, and their position in the file is their
position in the search: local accounts come first and keep working when the API
server does not.
.Pp
Then write
.Xr ypstns.conf 5 ,
check it, and start both daemons:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ypstns -n
# rcctl enable portmap ypstns ypbind
# rcctl start portmap ypstns ypbind
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr portmap 8
has to be running first:
.Nm
registers with it, and cannot serve anything if it is absent.
.Ss Checking it
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ypwhich
$ ypcat passwd
$ ypmatch alice passwd
$ getent passwd alice
$ id alice
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr ypcat 1
and
.Xr ypmatch 1
ask the server directly and
.Xr getent 1
goes through libc, which is the distinction worth having when something is
wrong: an answer from the first and not the second means the
.Sq +
lines or
.Xr ypbind 8
rather than this daemon.
.Pp
.Xr yppoll 8
prints the order number of a map, which here is the time the directory was last
fetched - the one place to see how stale what is being served actually is.
.Ss Maps
.Bl -tag -width master.passwd.byname -compact
.It Sy passwd.byname
.It Sy passwd.byuid
The directory with the password field masked to
.Sq * .
.It Sy master.passwd.byname
.It Sy master.passwd.byuid
The same, with the hash as the API served it.
Answered only to a request from a reserved port; see
.Sx SECURITY
below.
.It Sy group.byname
.It Sy group.bygid
.It Sy netid.byname
Every group id a user holds, as
.Ql unix.<uid>@<domain> .
.It Sy ypservers
This machine.
.El
.Pp
.Sy YPPROC_XFR
and
.Sy YPPROC_CLEAR
are refused.
Both belong to a world with map transfers in it, and there is no
.Xr dbm 3
file here to push or to reopen - the maps come from an HTTP API on a timer, so
honouring them would have
.Xr yppush 8
report a success that did not happen.
There are no slave servers.
.Ss SSH keys
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8
is installed alongside and has nothing to do with YP:
.Xr sshd 8
runs a command and reads its output.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
AuthorizedKeysCommand     /usr/local/bin/stns-key-wrapper
AuthorizedKeysCommandUser nobody
.Ed
.Sh SECURITY
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It YP is IPv4, the API need not be
Sun RPC over anything but IPv4 is not something libc's YP client will ask for,
so the service and the
.Ic allow
list in
.Xr ypstns.conf 5
are both IPv4 and an IPv6 entry there would be a promise this daemon cannot
keep.
The API the fetcher talks to is an ordinary HTTP endpoint and is under no such
constraint: an
.Ic api_endpoint
of
.Ql http://[::1]:1104/v1
works as well as any other.
.It The directory is not served to the network by default
Only this machine, plus whatever
.Ic allow
names in
.Xr ypstns.conf 5 .
.Nm
is nearly always run beside the
.Xr ypbind 8
that consumes it, and a YP server reachable from the network hands the whole
directory to anybody who can guess the domain name - which is not a secret and
never was.
.It The hashes need a reserved port
.Sy master.passwd.byname
and
.Sy master.passwd.byuid
are answered only when the request came from a port below 1024.
.Xr ypserv 8
makes the same distinction, and it is the only thing between an ordinary user
on a client machine and every hash in the directory.
It is a sound claim over the loopback and a weak one over a network, which is
the other reason for the paragraph above.
.It The fetcher is confined
It runs as
.Sy _ypstns ,
after
.Xr unveil 2
has reduced the filesystem to the resolver configuration, the trust store and
whatever TLS material
.Xr stns.conf 5
names, and after
.Xr pledge 2
has reduced it to
.Qq stdio rpath inet dns .
The server half is pledged to
.Qq stdio inet proc :
by the time it starts serving it will never open a file again, and the only
thing
.Qq proc
buys it is the
.Xr kill 2
that passes a signal on to the fetcher.
.It Two settings in stns.conf do not apply
.Ic query_wrapper
runs a command, and the fetcher is pledged without
.Qq exec ;
it is ignored, with a line in the log, rather than aborting the process the
first time a refresh comes round.
The on-disk cache is turned off, because the daemon already holds the whole
directory and keeping a second, staler copy would cost
.Qq wpath cpath
for something nothing reads.
Both still apply to
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 ,
which is a separate program with no pledge.
.El
.Sh RELOADING
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dv SIGHUP
Fetch the directory again at once, rather than waiting out
.Ic interval .
.Dq rcctl reload ypstns
sends it.
.Pp
It does
.Em not
re-read the configuration.
The server half has pledged not to open a file again, and the fetcher gave up
the privileges it would need to read
.Xr stns.conf 5
a second time - which is the point of both, and is why
.Dq rcctl restart ypstns
is the answer for anything else.
.It Dv SIGTERM , Dv SIGINT
Deregister from
.Xr portmap 8 ,
stop the fetcher and exit.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/share/examples/ypstns -compact
.It Pa /etc/ypstns.conf
This daemon's configuration.
.It Pa /etc/stns/client/stns.conf
The STNS API client configuration, shared with
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 .
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/ypstns
.It Pa /usr/local/share/examples/ypstns
Commented samples of both files.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Pp
.Nm
does not begin serving, and does not register with
.Xr portmap 8 ,
until the first fetch has succeeded.
A YP server that answers
.Sy YP_NOMAP
while it is starting tells its clients
.Sy passwd.byname
does not exist, and a client that believes that is a machine nobody can log in
to; being absent for a moment is the better failure.
For the same reason a later fetch that fails changes nothing - the previous
directory keeps being served.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check a configuration file before installing it, as an ordinary user:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ypstns -n -f ./ypstns.conf
.Ed
.Pp
Watch it work, without daemonizing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ypstns -d -v
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ypcat 1 ,
.Xr ypmatch 1 ,
.Xr ypwhich 1 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr getpwnam 3 ,
.Xr stns.conf 5 ,
.Xr ypstns.conf 5 ,
.Xr portmap 8 ,
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 ,
.Xr ypbind 8 ,
.Xr ypldap 8 ,
.Xr yppoll 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An zakinko Aq Mt zakinko@snowrabbit.org
.Sh CAVEATS
YP is a protocol from 1985 with no authentication worth the name and no
encryption at all.
Everything above about reserved ports and access lists is making the best of
that, not fixing it.
It is a reasonable arrangement on a machine talking to its own
.Xr ypbind 8
over the loopback and a poor one across a network you do not control.
